    "lawCode": "Add §§75-m & 111-d, Dom Rel L; add §§643 & 658, Fam Ct Act; add §393, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Domestic Relations Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4407\n\nSPONSOR: PARKER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the domestic relations law and the family court act, in\nrelation to prohibiting the making of decisions concerning guardianship,\ncustody or visitation or adoption petitions solely on the basis of a\nparent's, guardian's or custodian's blindness; and to amend the social\nservices law, in relation to prohibiting the department of social\nservices from denying, deciding or opposing a petition or request for\nguardianship, custody or visitation solely because the petitioner is\nblind and to prohibiting a local social services agency from taking\nactions solely because a parent, custodian or guardian is blind\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the domestic relations law and the family court act, in\nrelation to prohibiting the making of decisions concerning guardianship,\ncustody or visitation or adoption petitions solely on the basis of a\nparent's, guardian's or custodian's blindness; and to amend the social\nservices law, in relation to prohibiting the department of social\nservices from denying, deciding or opposing a petition or request for\nguardianship, custody or visitation solely because the petitioner is\nblind and to prohibiting a local social services agency from taking\nactions solely because a parent, custodian, or guardian is blind.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAll blind Americans have the right to found a family, to freely and\nresponsibly decide on the number and spacing of their children, and to\nretain the custody of their offspring on an equal basis with others.\nThis right to parent is rooted in the due process clause of the Four-\nteenth Amendment; however, blind people are often stripped of these\nconstitutional rights when statutes, judicial decisions, and child\nwelfare practices are based on the presumption that blindness automat-\nically means parental incompetence.\n\nThe presumption that blindness automatically means parental incompetence\nis a misconception. Given the proper tools and education, blindness can\nbe reduced to a physical nuisance. Because many sighted people do not\nunderstand the techniques that blind people use to accomplish everyday\ntasks, sighted judges, social workers, and state officials assume that\nthose tasks cannot be completed by a blind person. Using alternative\ntechniques, blind people are capable of living independent, productive\nlives, which include providing safe and loving homes for their children.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S.4536 - Passed Senate\n\n2017-18: 5.3266 - Passed the Senate died in Assembly Judiciary\n\n2015-16: S.4264 - Referred to Children and Families\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
